=========================================================
C++ std::array (Fixed-Size Sequence Container)
INTERVIEW NOTES (CLEAN & FINAL)
=========================================================

1. What is std::array?
----------------------
- std::array is a fixed-size sequence container.
- Introduced in C++11.
- Wrapper around a C-style array.
- Provides STL container interface.

Header:
#include <array>

Declaration:

std::array<int, 5> arr = {1,2,3,4,5};

-----------------------------------------

2. Internal Implementation
--------------------------
- Stores elements contiguously in memory.
- Size is fixed at compile time.
- No dynamic memory allocation.

Memory Layout:

+----+----+----+----+----+
| 10 | 20 | 30 | 40 | 50 |
+----+----+----+----+----+

Same contiguous layout as a C array.

-----------------------------------------

3. Why use std::array?
----------------------
✔ STL container interface
✔ Works with STL algorithms
✔ Knows its size
✔ Can be copied/assigned
✔ Safer than C arrays using at()

-----------------------------------------

4. Common Functions
-------------------
operator[]
at()
front()
back()

begin()
end()

size()
empty()

fill()
swap()

data()

-----------------------------------------

5. Complexity
-------------
operator[]      -> O(1)
at()            -> O(1)
front()         -> O(1)
back()          -> O(1)
size()          -> O(1)
empty()         -> O(1)
fill()          -> O(n)
swap()          -> O(n)
begin()/end()   -> O(1)

-----------------------------------------

6. Random Access
----------------
✔ Supported

Example:

arr[2]
arr.at(2)

Complexity:
O(1)

-----------------------------------------

7. Memory
---------
✔ Contiguous memory
✔ No heap allocation
✔ Fixed-size container

Unlike vector:
- Never reallocates
- Capacity == Size

-----------------------------------------

8. What is NOT supported?********************
-------------------------
❌ push_back()
❌ pop_back()
❌ insert()
❌ erase()
❌ resize()********************************

Reason:
Size is fixed at compile time.

-----------------------------------------

9. std::array vs C Array
------------------------

C Array
-------
- No size()
- No iterators
- Doesn't work naturally with STL algorithms
- Cannot be assigned directly

std::array
----------
- size()
- begin()/end()
- STL compatible
- Copyable
- Assignable
- at() provides bounds checking

-----------------------------------------

10. std::array vs std::vector
-----------------------------

std::array
-----------
✔ Fixed size
✔ No dynamic allocation
✔ Contiguous memory
✔ No reallocation
✔ Faster when size is known

std::vector
------------
✔ Dynamic size
✔ Heap allocation
✔ Can grow/shrink
✔ May reallocate memory

-----------------------------------------

11. Important Interview Questions
---------------------------------

Q. Is std::array contiguous?
Answer:
Yes.

---------------------------------

Q. Is std::array dynamic?***********
Answer:
No.
Its size is fixed at compile time.

---------------------------------

Q. Does std::array allocate memory on the heap?
Answer:
No.
Elements are stored inside the array object itself.

---------------------------------

Q. Can std::array grow?
Answer:
No.

---------------------------------

Q. Does std::array support random access?
Answer:
Yes.
operator[] and at() are O(1).

---------------------------------

Q. Why use std::array instead of a C array?

Answer:
- STL compatible
- size()
- begin()/end()
- at()
- Copyable
- Assignable
- Safer and easier to use

-----------------------------------------

12. One-line Interview Answer
-----------------------------
std::array is a fixed-size, contiguous sequence container introduced in 
C++11 that wraps a C-style array while providing the standard STL container interface.

